export const body = "<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>Consistency is among the most important qualities of video game animation. Since gameplay animations are repeated over and over, their timings have to remain unchanged. And to keep every action consistent, gameplay animators follow this three-phase sequence: anticipation, contact, and recovery.</p>\n<p>By allocating a certain amount of frames to each phase, animators can fine-tune and balance player actions. Therefore, this sequence is very prevalent in animating action games.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/9328a7393b700efc1e70.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/9328a7393b700efc1e70.webp\" width=\"1003\" height=\"492\" style=\"--source-width:255px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/a2a26b83d3d2bdfce3a5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/a2a26b83d3d2bdfce3a5.webp\" width=\"640\" height=\"250\" style=\"--source-width:255px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/6d9e33158d84899ee2fa.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/6d9e33158d84899ee2fa.webp\" width=\"523\" height=\"294\" style=\"--source-width:306px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/1ade89ade3c6d31afd26.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/1ade89ade3c6d31afd26.webp\" width=\"555\" height=\"312\" style=\"--source-width:306px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>The anticipation is the wind-up of an action, in this case an attack. From the visual standpoint, this phase helps the player anticipate the speed and power of their upcoming move. A heavy attack takes more time to prepare, and a light attack less time.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>In our Cookie about collision detection, we brought up the concept of hitbox, which is the damaging area of an attack. The contact phase is when the hitbox becomes active and able to hurt your enemy.</p>\n<p>This phase is usually the shortest one, but there are exceptions. For example, Dhalsim in <em>Street Fighter</em> can breathe fire that lasts many seconds, which means the attack has a really long contact phase.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/6cb15e3b24c7e2966df2.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/6cb15e3b24c7e2966df2.webp\" width=\"640\" height=\"352\" style=\"--source-width:255px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/369f6bfa820ad7462b40.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/369f6bfa820ad7462b40.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:255px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/22f718cbcf3c7d89c335.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/22f718cbcf3c7d89c335.webp\" width=\"480\" height=\"270\" style=\"--source-width:306px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/673567b82af949201df4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/673567b82af949201df4.webp\" width=\"525\" height=\"292\" style=\"--source-width:306px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Finally we have the recovery—the period in which the character returns to its neutral pose. Some animators liken this phase to the follow-through in baseball.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Of course, this three-phase sequence is not limited to just attack animations. They are quite universal, particularly with “anticipation” being 1 of 12 principles of animation. For example, this jump has 7 poses spanning over 3 phases:</p>\n<ol><li>Standing (idle/neutral state)</li><li>Anticipation</li><li>Jump (Contact)</li><li>Mid-air</li><li>Fall (Contact)</li><li>Recovery</li><li>Standing (idle/neutral state)</li></ol>\n<p>Ultimately, the goal of this sequence is to provide game animators with a consistent framework to pace the individual action within a loop motion.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/e7d239c342f90f4a0a6f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/e7d239c342f90f4a0a6f.webp\" width=\"480\" height=\"360\" style=\"--source-width:255px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>";
